window.__GUIDE_CHUNKS__.push([{"id": "02-016", "chapter_num": 2, "chapter_title": "Don't Die Slowly", "chapter_file": "02-dont-die-slowly.md", "number": 16, "title": "Stack stray bursts of effort — stairs, hurrying — to 4–5 minutes a day", "money": "0", "time": "low", "willpower": "some", "benefit": "high", "dimension": "death", "evidence": "A", "grade": "A", "disputed": false, "cost": "Free, no extra workout time. The hard part is remembering: take the stairs, walk fast, skip the shortcut.", "plain": "People who never exercise but get three one-to-two-minute bursts of vigorous daily activity run about 40% lower death and nearly half the cardiovascular death of people with none. Vigorous means stairs, brisk hurrying, carrying loads uphill.", "benefit_text": "Stamatakis et al. (2022), UK Biobank, 25,241 non-exercisers, 6.9 years, 852 deaths: any vigorous intermittent lifestyle activity vs none — all-cause and cancer death 38%–40% lower, cardiovascular death 48%–49% lower. 4.4 min/day total: 26%–30% lower all-cause, 32%–34% lower cardiovascular.", "sources": "Stamatakis E et al. (2022). Association of wearable device-measured vigorous intermittent lifestyle physical activity with mortality. Nature Medicine. <https://doi.org/10.1038/s41591-022-02100-x>", "notes": "Disputed: wearables beat questionnaires, but it's still follow-up, not a trial, with 6.9 years of tracking. Only applies to non-exercisers — regular trainers don't get this item.", "urls": ["https://doi.org/10.1038/s41591-022-02100-x"]}, {"id": "02-017", "chapter_num": 2, "chapter_title": "Don't Die Slowly", "chapter_file": "02-dont-die-slowly.md", "number": 17, "title": "30–60 minutes a week of strength training", "money": "0", "time": "mid", "willpower": "some", "benefit": "mid", "dimension": "death", "evidence": "A", "grade": "A", "disputed": false, "cost": "Bodyweight squats and push-ups are free. One to two sessions a week, 20–30 minutes each. The hard part is keeping it on the calendar.", "plain": "People doing 30–60 weekly minutes of strength work run 10–20% lower death than non-lifters. More is not better. Strength plus aerobic beats either alone.", "benefit_text": "Momma et al. (2022), meta-analysis of cohorts: strength training vs none — 10%–17% lower mortality; the biggest drop (10%–20%) at ~30–60 min/week, no extra benefit beyond. Strength + aerobic better than neither.", "sources": "Momma H et al. (2022). Muscle-strengthening activities are associated with lower risk and mortality in major non-communicable diseases. British Journal of Sports Medicine. <https://doi.org/10.1136/bjsports-2021-105061>", "notes": "Follow-up studies with self-reported exercise; the \"too much gets worse\" tail is weakly evidenced — don't cap training over it. For older adults, strength also prevents falls and preserves muscle (Chapter 1, item 13).", "urls": ["https://doi.org/10.1136/bjsports-2021-105061"]}, {"id": "02-018", "chapter_num": 2, "chapter_title": "Don't Die Slowly", "chapter_file": "02-dont-die-slowly.md", "number": 18, "title": "Don't sit for hours unbroken — get up and move periodically", "money": "0", "time": "low", "willpower": "some", "benefit": "high", "dimension": "death", "evidence": "A", "grade": "A", "disputed": false, "cost": "Free; standing up takes seconds. The hard part is remembering once you're sunk in — set a reminder.", "plain": "The most sedentary people run 2.6 times the death of the least sedentary; the longest unbroken sitters run about double the shortest. But 60–75 daily minutes of moderate activity essentially erases the excess.", "benefit_text": "Diaz et al. (2017), US cohort with accelerometers: most vs least total sedentary time HR 2.63 (about 2.6×); longest vs shortest uninterrupted bouts HR 1.96. Ekelund et al. (2016), 1M+ people: 8+ h/day sitting with almost no activity HR 1.59 vs <4 h + most active; with 60–75 min/day moderate activity, even 8+ h sitters showed HR 1.04 — no significant difference.", "sources": "Diaz KM et al. (2017). Patterns of Sedentary Behavior and Mortality in U.S. Middle-Aged and Older Adults. Annals of Internal Medicine. <https://doi.org/10.7326/M17-0212>; Ekelund U et al. (2016). Does physical activity attenuate, or even eliminate, the detrimental association of sitting time with mortality? Lancet. <https://doi.org/10.1016/S0140-6736(16)30370-1>", "notes": "The reliable takeaway: how much you move matters more than how long you sit. The 2.63× includes plenty of already-sick sitters, so it reads too high. Same review: 3+ h/day of TV kept its risk at every activity level (the most active only pushed the threshold to 5 h).", "urls": ["https://doi.org/10.1016/S0140-6736(16", "https://doi.org/10.7326/M17-0212"]}, {"id": "02-019", "chapter_num": 2, "chapter_title": "Don't Die Slowly", "chapter_file": "02-dont-die-slowly.md", "number": 19, "title": "Eat less processed meat (ham, bacon, sausage, luncheon meat)", "money": "0", "time": "low", "willpower": "some", "benefit": "high", "dimension": "death", "evidence": "A", "grade": "A", "disputed": false, "cost": "Free — it saves money, no time. The hard part is eating less of something you like.", "plain": "The heaviest processed-meat eaters run about 23% higher death than the lightest. Each extra daily serving runs about 23% higher too.", "benefit_text": "Larsson & Orsini (2014), meta-analysis: highest vs lowest processed meat — RR 1.23 (23% higher); red meat total RR 1.29; unprocessed red meat RR 1.10 (not significant). Schwingshackl et al. (2017): per daily serving, processed meat RR 1.23, red meat RR 1.10.", "sources": "Larsson SC, Orsini N (2014). Red meat and processed meat consumption and all-cause mortality: a meta-analysis. American Journal of Epidemiology. <https://doi.org/10.1093/aje/kwt261>; Johnston BC et al. (2019). Unprocessed Red Meat and Processed Meat Consumption: Dietary Guideline Recommendations From the NutriRECS Consortium. Annals of Internal Medicine. <https://doi.org/10.7326/M19-1621>", "notes": "Disputed: NutriRECS graded these studies low-certainty (GRADE) and gave only a weak \"keep eating as now\" recommendation. The fight is over how solid the evidence is, not its direction — no study finds processed meat beneficial. Unprocessed red meat's effect is small and not significant. **Canada:** Canada's Food Guide advises limiting processed meats.", "urls": ["https://doi.org/10.1093/aje/kwt261", "https://doi.org/10.7326/M19-1621"]}, {"id": "02-020", "chapter_num": 2, "chapter_title": "Don't Die Slowly", "chapter_file": "02-dont-die-slowly.md", "number": 20, "title": "Drink little or no alcohol", "money": "0", "time": "low", "willpower": "some", "benefit": "high", "dimension": "death", "evidence": "A", "grade": "A", "disputed": false, "cost": "Free — it saves money, no time. The hard part is the awkwardness of declining at social dinners.", "plain": "Past 100 g of pure alcohol a week (about 2.5 litres of 5% beer), the more you drink the earlier you die. At 40: 100–200 g/week costs about 6 months; 200–350 g costs 1–2 years; over 350 g costs 4–5 years. For total health, the best amount is zero.", "benefit_text": "Wood et al. (2018), 83 cohorts, 599,912 drinkers: lowest mortality at ≤100 g/week (~2.5 L of 5% beer or 300 mL of 40% spirits). Life lost at 40: ~6 months (100–200 g/week), 1–2 years (200–350 g), 4–5 years (>350 g). GBD 2016: the level minimizing total health loss is 0 drinks/week.", "sources": "Wood AM et al. (2018). Risk thresholds for alcohol consumption. Lancet. <https://doi.org/10.1016/S0140-6736(18)30134-X>; GBD 2016 Alcohol Collaborators (2018). Alcohol use and burden for 195 countries and territories. Lancet. <https://doi.org/10.1016/S0140-6736(18)31310-2>", "notes": "**Canada:** The Canadian Centre on Substance Use and Addiction (CCSA) guidance: no more than 2 standard drinks a week to keep risk low. Disputed: one 34-study pooling found up to 17–18% lower mortality at small amounts (men ≤4 drinks/day, women ≤2) — but its non-drinker group mixed in ex-drinkers and the already-ill; a bias-corrected pooling erased the protection. \"Start drinking for your health\" has no basis. Cutting back: item 22. Daily drinkers must not quit cold turkey: item 21.", "urls": ["https://doi.org/10.1016/S0140-6736(18"]}, {"id": "02-021", "chapter_num": 2, "chapter_title": "Don't Die Slowly", "chapter_file": "02-dont-die-slowly.md", "number": 21, "title": "Daily heavy drinker with shakes when you stop: don't quit cold turkey", "money": "low", "time": "mid", "willpower": "some", "benefit": "high", "dimension": "death", "evidence": "B", "grade": "B", "disputed": false, "cost": "A doctor or hospital visit — covered by OHIP. Severe cases need a few days admitted.", "plain": "Long-term heavy drinkers who stop suddenly risk withdrawal: tremor, sweating, racing heart, insomnia at the mild end — seizures, delirium tremens at the severe end, and that is a fatal emergency. In Norway's registry, 8.0% of the delirium-tremens group died each year. Tell your doctor exactly how much you drink daily.", "benefit_text": "Caputo et al. (2026) clinical review: alcohol withdrawal syndrome after abrupt stop or sharp cut — tremor, autonomic hyperactivity, anxiety, insomnia, perceptual disturbances, seizures, delirium tremens — \"common and potentially life-threatening\" in general medical care. Bramness et al. (2023), 36,287 Norwegians: annual mortality 8.0% (delirium tremens), 5.0% (withdrawal state), 3.6% (dependence); delirium-tremens SMR 9.8 (95% CI 8.9–10.7).", "sources": "Caputo F et al. (2026). Alcohol withdrawal syndrome in hospitalized patients: a practical review. European Journal of Internal Medicine. <https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ejim.2026.107103>; Bramness JG et al. (2023). Mortality and alcohol-related morbidity in patients with delirium tremens, alcohol withdrawal state or alcohol dependence in Norway. Addiction. <https://doi.org/10.1111/add.16297>", "notes": "**Ontario:** Go to the emergency department or your doctor — withdrawal is managed with medication plus vitamin B1 (thiamine). How to tell: drinking nearly daily, shaking/sweating/racing heart without a drink, needing a morning drink — any one of these means don't stop alone. Support: ConnexOntario at 1-866-531-2600. Graded B because the cohort describes people who already had delirium tremens, not people quitting cold turkey — the link is inference. You still quit — just in hospital. How much is too much: item 20. Cutting back: item 22.", "urls": ["https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ejim.2026.107103", "https://doi.org/10.1111/add.16297"]}, {"id": "02-022", "chapter_num": 2, "chapter_title": "Don't Die Slowly", "chapter_file": "02-dont-die-slowly.md", "number": 22, "title": "To drink less: count your weekly drinks first, then talk to your doctor for a few minutes", "money": "0", "time": "low", "willpower": "some", "benefit": "low", "dimension": "death", "evidence": "A", "grade": "A", "disputed": false, "cost": "Free. Log each drink daily; a few minutes with your doctor costs nothing extra.", "plain": "A few minutes of advice — reviewing your intake, laying out the risks, setting a target — meant about 20 g less pure alcohol a week a year later, roughly half a litre of beer. Longer sessions added nothing. Two medications help people who can't stop: about one extra person in 12 stays off heavy drinking.", "benefit_text": "Kaner et al. (2018), 34 randomized trials, 15,197 people: brief intervention vs minimal/no intervention — 20 g/week less alcohol at one year (95% CI 12–28 g), moderate-quality; longer counselling added no benefit. Jonas et al. (2014), 122 trials, 22,803 people: acamprosate NNT 12 (95% CI 8–26) for return to any drinking; oral naltrexone 50 mg/day NNT 12 (8–26) for return to heavy drinking.", "sources": "Kaner EF et al. (2018). Effectiveness of brief alcohol interventions in primary care populations. Cochrane Database of Systematic Reviews. <https://doi.org/10.1002/14651858.CD004148.pub4>; Jonas DE et al. (2014). Pharmacotherapy for adults with alcohol use disorders in outpatient settings. JAMA. <https://doi.org/10.1001/jama.2014.3628>", "notes": "Benefit graded small: the trials measured drinks, not deaths — a surrogate endpoint by this book's rules. The drug trials were mostly in already-dependent patients who also got counselling; the numbers are the extra benefit on top of that. Whether these drugs are indicated and available to you is your doctor's call — don't order them online. Already in withdrawal: item 21.", "urls": ["https://doi.org/10.1001/jama.2014.3628", "https://doi.org/10.1002/14651858.CD004148.pub4"]}, {"id": "02-023", "chapter_num": 2, "chapter_title": "Don't Die Slowly", "chapter_file": "02-dont-die-slowly.md", "number": 23, "title": "A small handful of nuts a day", "money": "low", "time": "low", "willpower": "no", "benefit": "high", "dimension": "death", "evidence": "A", "grade": "A", "disputed": false, "cost": "28 g a day, roughly $200–$300 a year. Eat them as-is, no time.", "plain": "Regular nut eaters run about 20% lower death than non-eaters. Once a week already shows about 11% lower; a daily handful shows the most.", "benefit_text": "Bao et al. (2013), two US cohorts, 119,000 people: vs never — <1/week HR 0.93 (95% CI 0.90–0.96), 1/week 0.89 (11% lower), 2–4/week 0.87, 5–6/week 0.85, 7+/week 0.80 (0.73–0.86, 20% lower).", "sources": "Bao Y, Han J, Hu FB, et al. (2013). Association of nut consumption with total and cause-specific mortality. New England Journal of Medicine. <https://doi.org/10.1056/NEJMoa1307352>", "notes": "Buy plain, unsalted. Nuts are calorie-dense — don't graze freely. Follow-up data only: nut eaters live healthier overall, so discount the numbers.", "urls": ["https://doi.org/10.1056/NEJMoa1307352"]}, {"id": "02-024", "chapter_num": 2, "chapter_title": "Don't Die Slowly", "chapter_file": "02-dont-die-slowly.md", "number": 24, "title": "Swap some red meat for fish and poultry", "money": "0", "time": "low", "willpower": "some", "benefit": "low", "dimension": "death", "evidence": "A", "grade": "A", "disputed": false, "cost": "Free, no time. This swaps one meat for another — not extra spending. The hard part is a small taste adjustment.", "plain": "Two extra servings a week of processed and red meat runs about 3% higher death; the same servings as fish, poultry show no increase. This effect is small — a no-effort swap, not something to agonize over.", "benefit_text": "Zhong et al. (2020), 6 US cohorts pooled, ~30,000 people: per 2 extra servings/week — processed meat HR 1.03 (3% higher, 95% CI 1.02–1.05), unprocessed red meat HR 1.03 (3% higher, 1.01–1.05); poultry HR 0.99 (0.97–1.02), fish HR 0.99 (0.97–1.01) — neither significant.", "sources": "Zhong VW et al. (2020). Associations of Processed Meat, Unprocessed Red Meat, Poultry, or Fish Intake With Incident Cardiovascular Disease and All-Cause Mortality. JAMA Internal Medicine. <https://doi.org/10.1001/jamainternmed.2019.6969>", "notes": "Two servings a week moves the needle very little — don't expect extra years from the swap. Same money, pick fish and poultry first.", "urls": ["https://doi.org/10.1001/jamainternmed.2019.6969"]}, {"id": "02-025", "chapter_num": 2, "chapter_title": "Don't Die Slowly", "chapter_file": "02-dont-die-slowly.md", "number": 25, "title": "Swap some white rice and refined flour for whole grains", "money": "low", "time": "low", "willpower": "some", "benefit": "mid", "dimension": "death", "evidence": "A", "grade": "A", "disputed": false, "cost": "Brown rice, oats, and whole-wheat flour cost a little more; no extra time. The hard part is adjusting to the texture.", "plain": "People eating 90 g more whole grains a day run about 17% lower death — 90 g is about three servings. The benefit keeps growing to about 210–225 g a day.", "benefit_text": "Aune et al. (2016), dose-response meta-analysis: per 90 g/day more whole grains — RR 0.83 (17% lower); benefit continues to 210–225 g/day. Schwingshackl et al. (2017): per serving RR 0.92.", "sources": "Aune D et al. (2016). Whole grain consumption and risk of cardiovascular disease, cancer, and all cause and cause specific mortality. BMJ. <https://doi.org/10.1136/bmj.i2716>", "notes": "No need to switch everything — half swapped already lands in the best-value stretch. Follow-up data only: whole-grain eaters were healthier to begin with, inflating the numbers, and studies disagree widely (I² 83%). **Canada:** Canada's Food Guide — make a quarter of your plate whole grains. Benefit graded mid (17% < 20% threshold), not high.", "urls": ["https://doi.org/10.1136/bmj.i2716"]}, {"id": "02-026", "chapter_num": 2, "chapter_title": "Don't Die Slowly", "chapter_file": "02-dont-die-slowly.md", "number": 26, "title": "Tea three or more times a week", "money": "low", "time": "low", "willpower": "no", "benefit": "mid", "dimension": "death", "evidence": "A", "grade": "A", "disputed": false, "cost": "Tens of dollars a year. Minutes to brew.", "plain": "Habitual tea drinkers run about 15% lower death. From age 50, they average 1.26 extra years of life and 1.41 extra years free of atherosclerotic cardiovascular disease.", "benefit_text": "Wang et al. (2020), China-PAR, 100,900 people: habitual tea drinking — HR 0.85 (15% lower, 95% CI 0.79–0.90); from age 50: +1.26 years life expectancy, +1.41 years free of atherosclerotic cardiovascular disease.", "sources": "Wang X, Liu F, Li J, et al. (2020). Tea consumption and the risk of atherosclerotic cardiovascular disease and all-cause mortality: The China-PAR project. European Journal of Preventive Cardiology. <https://doi.org/10.1177/2047487319894685>", "notes": "Disputed: follow-up data only, and Chinese male tea drinkers smoke and drink more — partly adjusted, never fully. Don't drink it scalding: item 31. Mouldy tea: store dry, discard damp tea. Green-tea extract capsules are different — concentrated catechins in one gulp can injure the liver; brewed tea doesn't do that. This item means brewed tea only.", "urls": ["https://doi.org/10.1177/2047487319894685"]}, {"id": "02-027", "chapter_num": 2, "chapter_title": "Don't Die Slowly", "chapter_file": "02-dont-die-slowly.md", "number": 27, "title": "Three to four cups of coffee a day, black with no sugar", "money": "low", "time": "low", "willpower": "no", "benefit": "mid", "dimension": "death", "evidence": "A", "grade": "A", "disputed": false, "cost": "Home-brewed, $1–$2 a day. Minutes per cup.", "plain": "People drinking three to four cups a day run about 17% lower death than non-drinkers. Black — sugar and cream caps cancel the benefit.", "benefit_text": "Poole et al. (2017), umbrella review of 201 meta-analyses: 3–4 cups/day vs none — RR 0.83 (17% lower, 95% CI 0.79–0.88).", "sources": "Poole R, Kennedy OJ, Roderick P, et al. (2017). Coffee consumption and health: umbrella review of meta-analyses of multiple health outcomes. BMJ. <https://doi.org/10.1136/bmj.j5024>", "notes": "Disputed: the authors state the evidence is almost entirely observational and call for proper randomized trials before claiming causation. Pregnancy, arrhythmia, anxiety, insomnia — skip this one. Benefit graded mid (17% < 20% threshold), not high.", "urls": ["https://doi.org/10.1136/bmj.j5024"]}, {"id": "02-028", "chapter_num": 2, "chapter_title": "Don't Die Slowly", "chapter_file": "02-dont-die-slowly.md", "number": 28, "title": "Five servings (about 400 g) of fruit and vegetables a day", "money": "low", "time": "mid", "willpower": "some", "benefit": "mid", "dimension": "death", "evidence": "A", "grade": "A", "disputed": false, "cost": "A few dollars a day. Daily washing and chopping takes time. The hard part is buying and eating enough, every day.", "plain": "Each extra 200 g a day runs about 10% lower death. Five servings a day runs about 13% lower than two. Two fruit plus three vegetables is the sweet spot — more adds nothing.", "benefit_text": "Aune et al. (2017), dose-response meta-analysis: per 200 g/day — RR 0.90 (10% lower); benefit to 800 g/day. Wang et al. (2021), 2 US cohorts + 26-study meta-analysis: 5 vs 2 servings/day — HR 0.87 (13% lower); best at 2 fruit + 3 vegetables, no further drop above.", "sources": "Aune D et al. (2017). Fruit and vegetable intake and the risk of cardiovascular disease, total cancer and all-cause mortality. International Journal of Epidemiology. <https://doi.org/10.1093/ije/dyw319>; Wang DD et al. (2021). Fruit and Vegetable Intake and Mortality. Circulation. <https://doi.org/10.1161/CIRCULATIONAHA.120.048996>", "notes": "Five is enough — both studies agree ten buys nothing extra. Follow-up data only: big-vegetable eaters differ in income, education, and exercise too, so \"10% lower\" is already an upper bound. **Canada:** Canada's Food Guide — half your plate vegetables and fruit.", "urls": ["https://doi.org/10.1093/ije/dyw319", "https://doi.org/10.1161/CIRCULATIONAHA.120.048996"]}, {"id": "02-029", "chapter_num": 2, "chapter_title": "Don't Die Slowly", "chapter_file": "02-dont-die-slowly.md", "number": 29, "title": "Eat less ultra-processed food (chips, instant noodles, pastries, ready meals)", "money": "0", "time": "high", "willpower": "yes", "benefit": "high", "dimension": "death", "evidence": "A", "grade": "A", "disputed": false, "cost": "Free to swap; cooking from less-processed ingredients takes daily time. The hard part is years of resisting convenience.", "plain": "The heaviest ultra-processed eaters run about 21% higher death and 50% higher cardiovascular death than the lightest. But this evidence is graded low-certainty — read the numbers discounted.", "benefit_text": "Lane et al. (2024), umbrella review: high vs low ultra-processed intake — all-cause RR 1.21 (21% higher), cardiovascular death RR 1.50 (50% higher) (\"highly suggestive\" and \"convincing\" evidence grades); GRADE certainty low to very low.", "sources": "Lane MM et al. (2024). Ultra-processed food exposure and adverse health outcomes: umbrella review of epidemiological meta-analyses. BMJ. <https://doi.org/10.1136/bmj-2023-077310>", "notes": "Disputed on three counts: the NOVA classification lumps nutritionally different foods together; it overlaps heavily with sugary drinks and processed meat (items 7 and 19), so the independent effect is unclear; and GRADE rates the certainty low. Do items 7 and 19 first, then consider this one.", "urls": ["https://doi.org/10.1136/bmj-2023-077310"]}, {"id": "02-030", "chapter_num": 2, "chapter_title": "Don't Die Slowly", "chapter_file": "02-dont-die-slowly.md", "number": 30, "title": "Don't cook or heat with coal and wood — switch to electric or gas", "money": "high", "time": "low", "willpower": "no", "benefit": "high", "dimension": "death", "evidence": "A", "grade": "A", "disputed": false, "cost": "A stove upgrade plus fuel runs hundreds to thousands of dollars — a one-time cost, no ongoing time. (City households are already on electric or gas.)", "plain": "Cooking with coal and wood runs about 11% higher death; heating with it about 14% higher. Households that switched to clean fuels ran 13–33% lower than those still burning.", "benefit_text": "Yu et al. (2018), 271,000 Chinese adults without cardiovascular disease, follow-up: solid-fuel cooking vs clean — HR 1.11 (11% higher); solid-fuel heating — HR 1.14 (14% higher). Switched to clean fuels vs still burning: cooking HR 0.87 (13% lower), heating HR 0.67 (33% lower).", "sources": "Yu K et al. (2018). Association of Solid Fuel Use With Risk of Cardiovascular and All-Cause Mortality in Rural China. JAMA. <https://doi.org/10.1001/jama.2018.2151>", "notes": "**Canada:** Replace old uncertified wood stoves with EPA/CSA-certified models or heat pumps; burn only dry, seasoned wood; never run generators or barbecues indoors (carbon monoxide). Follow-up data only: switchers were also wealthier, so the 0.67 figure mixes in affluence. Outdoor PM2.5 is largely beyond personal control — moving, masks, and purifiers are the options, and purifiers have no mortality trials.", "urls": ["https://doi.org/10.1001/jama.2018.2151"]}, {"id": "02-031", "chapter_num": 2, "chapter_title": "Don't Die Slowly", "chapter_file": "02-dont-die-slowly.md", "number": 31, "title": "Don't drink, eat, or bathe in water hotter than about 65°C (maté, very hot tea, porridge, hot springs)", "money": "0", "time": "low", "willpower": "some", "benefit": "high", "dimension": "death", "evidence": "A", "grade": "A", "disputed": false, "cost": "Free. A little patience to let food cool.", "plain": "People drinking \"very hot\" maté run 4.1 times the oesophageal cancer of non-drinkers; hot (not very hot) is about double. Very hot drinks — hot tea and others — run about 90% higher oesophageal cancer. The \"65°C\" line comes from IARC's official classification: drinking anything above 65°C is \"probably carcinogenic to humans\".", "benefit_text": "Lubin et al. (2014), case-control: very hot maté OR 4.14 (about 4.1×, 95% CI 3.25–5.29); hot 1.81 (1.42–2.31); warm 1.00 — figures UNVERIFIED in this edition (cited DOI does not resolve; see docs/verification/link-sweep.md). Islami et al. (2009), systematic review: hot beverages — oesophageal squamous cell carcinoma RR 1.91 (about 90% higher, 95% CI 1.64–2.23). IARC Monographs: \"very hot beverages above 65°C\" — Group 2A, probably carcinogenic.", "sources": "Lubin JH et al. (2014). Maté intake and risk of oesophageal squamous cell carcinoma in Uruguay. Cancer Epidemiology, Biomarkers & Prevention. DOI 10.1158/1055-9965.EPI-13-0346 — UNVERIFIED (does not resolve as of 2026-10-01); IARC Monographs Working Group (2016). Very hot beverages at or above 65°C are a probable cause of cancer of the oesophagus. <https://www.iarc.who.int/wp-content/uploads/2018/07/pr244_E.pdf>", "notes": "Group 2A is probability, not a measured effect size — so Benefit carries no exact number. Case-control evidence is graded B here (recall bias); IARC's official classification pulls the item grade to A. \"Hot\" vs \"very hot\" came from each participant's own description — no thermometer in the studies. Practical rule: if it burns your lip, wait; a thermometer costs under $20. **Ontario:** Hot tubs and saunas — follow the facility's posted limits. Bathing water this hot can also burn skin — keep hot water tanks at or below 60°C against scalds, and never pour near-boiling water on skin.", "urls": ["https://www.iarc.who.int/wp-content/uploads/2018/07/pr244_E.pdf"]}, {"id": "02-032", "chapter_num": 2, "chapter_title": "Don't Die Slowly", "chapter_file": "02-dont-die-slowly.md", "number": 32, "title": "When brushing and flossing, also brush your tongue", "money": "0", "time": "low", "willpower": "no", "benefit": "mid", "dimension": "death", "evidence": "B", "grade": "B", "disputed": false, "cost": "Free. A few seconds of tongue brushing on top of what you already do.", "plain": "People who brush their teeth three-plus times a day run about 14% lower heart failure; tongue cleaning may help too, though this evidence is graded low — read the numbers discounted. Tongue brushing cuts the bacteria that give bad breath by 53–75% in the best studies.", "benefit_text": "Chang et al. (2020), 161,286 Koreans: tooth brushing ≥3/day vs 0–1 — HR 0.86 (14% lower, 95% CI 0.82–0.90) for heart failure; also fewer cardiovascular events and deaths. Pedrazzi et al. (2004), randomized: tongue brushing cut volatile sulphur compounds 53–75%.", "sources": "Chang Y et al. (2020). Improved oral hygiene care is associated with decreased risk of new-onset atrial fibrillation and heart failure. European Journal of Preventive Cardiology. <https://doi.org/10.1177/2047487319886018>; Pedrazzi V et al. (2004). Tongue-cleaning methods: a comparative clinical trial employing a toothbrush and a tongue scraper. Special Care in Dentistry. <https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1754-4505.2004.tb01694.x>", "notes": "Disputed: the heart-failure finding is observational — frequent brushers differ in every health behaviour. The breath trial was randomized but tiny (10 people) and short. This item's practical payload is oral health and breath, not years of life — treat the 14% as suggestive, not a promise. Benefit graded mid (14% < 20%).", "urls": ["https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1754-4505.2004.tb01694.x", "https://doi.org/10.1177/2047487319886018"]}, {"id": "02-033", "chapter_num": 2, "chapter_title": "Don't Die Slowly", "chapter_file": "02-dont-die-slowly.md", "number": 33, "title": "Take a full two-day weekend every week, with no overtime", "money": "0", "time": "high", "willpower": "yes", "benefit": "high", "dimension": "death", "evidence": "B", "grade": "B", "disputed": false, "cost": "Free in money; costs you two full days a week. The hard part is turning down overtime and career pressure.", "plain": "The WHO and ILO attribute 745,000 deaths a year worldwide to working 55-plus hours a week. In China's national data, people with a two-day weekend and no overtime ran 41% lower death than people with no days off and overtime. The gap shrank after adjusting for everything else, so discount it.", "benefit_text": "Pega et al. (2021), WHO/ILO: working ≥55 h/week vs 35–40 h — stroke RR 1.35 (95% CI 1.13–1.61), ischaemic heart disease RR 1.17 (1.05–1.31); 745,194 deaths (about 745,000) attributable to long hours in 2016. Wang (2025), 29,000 Chinese workers: two-day weekend without overtime vs no rest with overtime — mortality HR 0.59 (41% lower, 95% CI 0.36–0.97); adjusted HR 0.71 (0.44–1.15), no longer significant.", "sources": "Pega F et al. (2021). Global, regional, and national burdens of ischemic heart disease and stroke attributable to exposure to long working hours. Environment International. <https://doi.org/10.1016/j.envint.2021.106595>; Wang P et al. (2025). Association between rest days and all-cause mortality in China: a nationally representative cohort. PMC. <https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12311587/>", "notes": "Disputed: the Chinese \"41% lower\" fades to nothing after full adjustment — the strongest claim is only about the ≥55-hour threshold (15% more stroke). If overtime is forced on you, that's the next chapter's domain — overwork protection is in 06 (labour) and the money chapters. Graded B: the rest-day numbers are single-cohort and adjusted-away.", "urls": ["https://doi.org/10.1016/j.envint.2021.106595", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12311587/"]}, {"id": "02-034", "chapter_num": 2, "chapter_title": "Don't Die Slowly", "chapter_file": "02-dont-die-slowly.md", "number": 34, "title": "Don't sit or lie down all day — get up and move every half hour", "money": "0", "time": "low", "willpower": "no", "benefit": "high", "dimension": "death", "evidence": "A", "grade": "A", "disputed": false, "cost": "Free. No extra time — just standing up more often.", "plain": "People sitting 13.2+ hours a day ran about 2.63 times the death of people sitting under 6.5 hours — and this is after adjusting for exercise, so moving half an hour in the evening doesn't cancel a sedentary day. Regular movement breaks cut the blood-sugar and blood-pressure spikes that sitting causes.", "benefit_text": "Diaz et al. (2017), US cohort: sedentary ≥13.2 h/day vs <6.5 h — HR 2.63 (95% CI 1.60–4.30), adjusted for physical activity. Ekelund et al. (2016): high activity attenuated but did not eliminate the sedentary risk.", "sources": "Diaz KM et al. (2017). Patterns of Sedentary Behavior and Mortality in U.S. Middle-Aged and Older Adults. Annals of Internal Medicine. <https://doi.org/10.7326/M17-0212>; Ekelund U et al. (2016). Does physical activity attenuate, or even eliminate, the detrimental association of sitting time with mortality? The Lancet. <https://doi.org/10.1016/S0140-6736(16)30370-1>", "notes": "Standing all day has its own problems (veins, back) — the point is alternating, not standing still. If you stand for work, sit-breaks count too.", "urls": ["https://doi.org/10.1016/S0140-6736(16", "https://doi.org/10.7326/M17-0212"]}, {"id": "02-035", "chapter_num": 2, "chapter_title": "Don't Die Slowly", "chapter_file": "02-dont-die-slowly.md", "number": 35, "title": "Dairy: milk and yoghurt daily, skip the cream and sweetened drinks", "money": "low", "time": "low", "willpower": "no", "benefit": "mid", "dimension": "death", "evidence": "B", "grade": "B", "disputed": false, "cost": "A glass of milk or a tub of yoghurt a day — tens of dollars a month. Minutes.", "plain": "People with the highest dairy intake run about 17% lower death; the benefit concentrates in milk and yoghurt, not cream, cheese. This is follow-up data only — read it discounted. (Effect figure UNVERIFIED in this edition — see Benefit.)", "benefit_text": "A dose-response meta-analysis is cited for: high vs low dairy — RR 0.83 (17% lower) for all-cause mortality, with milk and yoghurt driving the benefit — UNVERIFIED in this edition: the cited DOI does not resolve to the described paper (see docs/verification/link-sweep.md).", "sources": "UNVERIFIED — the citation as written (O'Sullivan TA et al. 2013, Advances in Nutrition, doi:10.3945/an.112.003079) does not match any resolvable paper; the real O'Sullivan et al. 2013 is on saturated-fat sources and mortality (Am J Public Health) <https://doi.org/10.2105/AJPH.2013.301492>, a different topic. See docs/verification/link-sweep.md.", "notes": "Disputed: follow-up data only, and the literature disagrees — some cohorts show nothing, and Mendelian-randomization studies question whether dairy is causal at all. If you're lactose intolerant or vegan, calcium-fortified soy drink covers the nutrient. Benefit graded mid (17% < 20%), not high. Evidence downgraded A→B in the Phase 3 link sweep: the cited source does not support the claimed numbers.", "urls": ["https://doi.org/10.2105/AJPH.2013.301492"]}, {"id": "02-036", "chapter_num": 2, "chapter_title": "Don't Die Slowly", "chapter_file": "02-dont-die-slowly.md", "number": 36, "title": "Make sure you get some direct sunlight every day (without burning)", "money": "0", "time": "low", "willpower": "no", "benefit": "low", "dimension": "death", "evidence": "B", "grade": "B", "disputed": false, "cost": "Free. Minutes — the time you'd spend outside anyway.", "plain": "People avoiding the sun run about double the death of the most sun-exposed group — the link runs through vitamin D and physical activity, so read it discounted. Ten to fifteen minutes a day in summer is enough for most people.", "benefit_text": "Lindqvist et al. (2014), 29,518 Swedish women, 20 years: sun avoiders vs most active sun seekers — HR 2.0 (95% CI 1.6–2.5); non-smoking sun avoiders had similar life expectancy to smokers with the most sun.", "sources": "Lindqvist PG et al. (2014). Avoidance of sun exposure is a risk factor for all-cause mortality. Journal of Internal Medicine. <https://doi.org/10.1111/joim.12251>", "notes": "Graded B: single Swedish-women cohort, and sun habits correlate with exercise and outdoor life — the number is inflated. Sunburn still causes melanoma: avoid burning, never use tanning beds. **Canada:** October to March, the sun is too weak for vitamin D north of most of Ontario — diet or supplements carry winter. Sunscreen on the face, short unprotected time on arms and legs, is a reasonable compromise.", "urls": ["https://doi.org/10.1111/joim.12251"]}, {"id": "02-037", "chapter_num": 2, "chapter_title": "Don't Die Slowly", "chapter_file": "02-dont-die-slowly.md", "number": 37, "title": "Every year, get the flu shot (and your COVID booster)", "money": "low", "time": "mid", "willpower": "no", "benefit": "high", "dimension": "death", "evidence": "A", "grade": "A", "disputed": false, "cost": "Free at pharmacies and clinics across Ontario. The time is a pharmacy trip.", "plain": "In adults 65 and over, flu vaccination was linked to lower death in flu season in a large observational study (exact figure UNVERIFIED in this edition). In the big Danish trial, getting the flu shot in the two weeks after a heart attack ran 28% lower death a year later.", "benefit_text": "Nichol et al. (2003), adults ≥65: vaccination linked to lower all-cause mortality in flu season — specific figures previously stated here (713,872 person-seasons; OR 0.56) UNVERIFIED in this edition (see docs/verification/link-sweep.md). Fröbert et al. (2021, IAMI), 2,571 post-MI patients, randomized double-blind: flu vaccine within 14 days — HR 0.72 (about 28% lower, 95% CI 0.52–0.99) for all-cause death at one year.", "sources": "Nichol KL et al. (2003). Influenza vaccination and reduction in hospitalizations for cardiac disease and stroke among the elderly. New England Journal of Medicine. <https://doi.org/10.1056/NEJMoa025028>; Fröbert O et al. (2021). Influenza Vaccination after Myocardial Infarction: A Randomized, Double-Blind, Placebo-Controlled, Multicenter Trial. Circulation. <https://doi.org/10.1161/CIRCULATIONAHA.121.057042>", "notes": "**Ontario:** Flu shots are free at pharmacies and primary-care clinics each fall; COVID boosters per current NACI guidance. Nichol's 47% is observational — vaccinated seniors are healthier overall, so discount it; IAMI was randomized but in post-heart-attack patients, not the general public.", "urls": ["https://doi.org/10.1056/NEJMoa025028", "https://doi.org/10.1161/CIRCULATIONAHA.121.057042"]}, {"id": "02-038", "chapter_num": 2, "chapter_title": "Don't Die Slowly", "chapter_file": "02-dont-die-slowly.md", "number": 38, "title": "Women and girls: the HPV vaccine against cervical cancer", "money": "0", "time": "low", "willpower": "no", "benefit": "mid", "dimension": "death", "evidence": "A", "grade": "A", "disputed": false, "cost": "Free through school programs in Ontario. Catch-up doses cost money privately.", "plain": "Girls vaccinated before age 17 run about 88% lower cervical cancer. Vaccination at 17–30 still runs about 53% lower.", "benefit_text": "Lei et al. (2020), Sweden, 1,672,983 girls and women: vaccination before 17 — IRR 0.12 (88% lower, 95% CI 0.00–0.34); at 17–30 — IRR 0.47 (53% lower, 0.27–0.75).", "sources": "Lei J et al. (2020). HPV Vaccination and the Risk of Invasive Cervical Cancer. New England Journal of Medicine. <https://doi.org/10.1056/NEJMoa1917338>", "notes": "**Ontario:** Gardasil-9 is given free in grade 7 through school programs; catch-up is free only to age 26 under some criteria — check with your public health unit. Boys get it too (throat and anal cancers) — see ch1 item 27. Benefit graded mid: cervical cancer is one cancer among many, so the all-death effect is smaller than the 88%.", "urls": ["https://doi.org/10.1056/NEJMoa1917338"]}, {"id": "02-039", "chapter_num": 2, "chapter_title": "Don't Die Slowly", "chapter_file": "02-dont-die-slowly.md", "number": 39, "title": "25 and up: cervical cancer screening every five years", "money": "0", "time": "low", "willpower": "no", "benefit": "mid", "dimension": "death", "evidence": "A", "grade": "A", "disputed": false, "cost": "Free through Ontario's screening program. One appointment every five years.", "plain": "Women screened every five years run about 83% lower cervical cancer death than unscreened women. Every three years runs about 91% lower — the program's five-year schedule is already near the ceiling.", "benefit_text": "Peirson et al. (2013), systematic review for the Canadian Task Force: screening — OR 0.35 (95% CI 0.30–0.41) for cervical cancer incidence; every 5 years vs never — OR 0.17 (83% lower); every 3 years vs never — OR 0.09 (91% lower).", "sources": "Peirson L et al. (2013). Screening for cervical cancer: a systematic review and meta-analysis. Systematic Reviews. <https://doi.org/10.1186/2046-4053-2-35>", "notes": "**Ontario:** Since March 2025, Ontario uses primary HPV testing from age 25 every 5 years — your doctor or a self-collection kit, no Pap needed unless the HPV test is positive. The numbers above come from Pap-era studies; HPV testing is at least as good. HPV vaccine: item 38.", "urls": ["https://doi.org/10.1186/2046-4053-2-35"]}, {"id": "02-040", "chapter_num": 2, "chapter_title": "Don't Die Slowly", "chapter_file": "02-dont-die-slowly.md", "number": 40, "title": "Organ donation: register as a donor, and tell your family", "money": "high", "time": "mid", "willpower": "yes", "benefit": "high", "dimension": "death", "evidence": "C", "grade": "C", "disputed": false, "cost": "Registering costs nothing but the thought of your own death; the tag on the item reflects the emotional cost. One registration, a few minutes, plus one hard conversation.", "plain": "One donor can save up to eight lives. In Ontario, you register your consent and — just as important — tell your family your wishes, because they are asked to confirm at the bedside.", "benefit_text": "One deceased donor provides organs for up to eight transplant recipients (Trillium Gift of Life Network). Registration alone is not enough: families are approached to affirm consent, and donation proceeds only with that affirmation in practice.", "sources": "Trillium Gift of Life Network. BeADonor.ca — register your consent for organ and tissue donation. <https://www.beadonor.ca>; Ontario, Trillium Gift of Life Network Act, S.O. 2000, c. 39, ss. 4–5 (consent by the deceased). <https://www.ontario.ca/laws/statute/00t39>", "notes": "**Ontario:** Register at beadonor.ca or when renewing your health card or driver's licence; consent under ss. 4–5 of the Trillium Gift of Life Network Act is binding, but in practice coordinators still ask the family — the conversation is the part that makes it happen. Evidence graded C: no mortality trial is possible — the \"benefit\" is lives saved for others, counted from transplant registry data. Selling organs is a criminal offence in Canada — this item is donation only. After you die: item 41.", "urls": ["https://www.beadonor.ca", "https://www.ontario.ca/laws/statute/00t39"]}, {"id": "02-041", "chapter_num": 2, "chapter_title": "Don't Die Slowly", "chapter_file": "02-dont-die-slowly.md", "number": 41, "title": "A living will: write down your end-of-life wishes while you're healthy", "money": "0", "time": "low", "willpower": "some", "benefit": "mid", "dimension": "death", "evidence": "C", "grade": "C", "disputed": false, "cost": "Free. An hour of thinking and writing, plus the conversation with family.", "plain": "Writing down what treatments you want — and don't want — when you can no longer speak means your wishes get followed instead of guessed. It also spares your family from making those decisions blind.", "benefit_text": "No mortality trial exists or could exist — the benefit is autonomy: wishes documented in advance are followed far more often than wishes never written down, and families report less distress and conflict when a directive exists.", "sources": "Ontario, Health Care Consent Act, 1996, S.O. 1996, c. 2, Sched. A (substitute decision-making and advance care planning framework). <https://www.ontario.ca/laws/statute/96h02>", "notes": "**Ontario:** Called advance care planning — name a substitute decision-maker and write your wishes; hospitals and long-term care homes must follow the Health Care Consent Act hierarchy. Free workbooks at advancecareplanning.ca. Evidence graded C: the payoff is dignity and family burden, not years of life. Organ donation: item 40.", "urls": ["https://www.ontario.ca/laws/statute/96h02"]}, {"id": "03-001", "chapter_num": 3, "chapter_title": "Don't waste your energy", "chapter_file": "03-dont-waste-energy.md", "number": 1, "title": "Sleep 7 to 8 hours a night; stop treating 6 as enough", "money": "0", "time": "high", "willpower": "some", "benefit": "high", "dimension": "time", "evidence": "A", "grade": "A", "disputed": false, "cost": "Free. It costs 1 to 2 hours of the day, moved into sleep. Going to bed earlier takes some resolve.", "plain": "Two weeks of six-hour nights drags performance down to the level of two straight nights without sleep. The catch is that people cannot feel it: their own sleepiness ratings stop rising after a few days, while their performance keeps falling.", "benefit_text": "48 healthy adults aged 21 to 38 slept 6 hours a night for 14 days. Their cognitive performance fell cumulatively to the level of 2 straight nights without sleep. Their self-rated sleepiness rose only in the first few days, then flatlined; the 6-hour and 4-hour groups rated themselves equally sleepy. This was a randomized controlled trial. Separately, pooling 70 studies of short-term sleep loss: lapses in simple attention g = -0.776, reasoning accuracy g = -0.125.", "sources": "Van Dongen, Maislin, Mullington & Dinges (2003). The cumulative cost of additional wakefulness. Sleep. <https://doi.org/10.1093/sleep/26.2.117>; Lim & Dinges (2010). A meta-analysis of the impact of short-term sleep deprivation on cognitive variables. Psychological Bulletin. <https://doi.org/10.1037/a0018883>", "notes": "Not feeling sleepy is not evidence of having slept enough. People who habitually sleep 6 hours only feel used to it; their performance does not catch up. The pooled part covers whole nights missed. Only one randomized trial supports chronic short sleep.", "urls": ["https://doi.org/10.1037/a0018883", "https://doi.org/10.1093/sleep/26.2.117"]}, {"id": "03-002", "chapter_num": 3, "chapter_title": "Don't waste your energy", "chapter_file": "03-dont-waste-energy.md", "number": 2, "title": "Skip coffee after lunch; keep it to the morning", "money": "0", "time": "low", "willpower": "some", "benefit": "high", "dimension": "time", "evidence": "A", "grade": "A", "disputed": false, "cost": "Free. For an 11 p.m. bedtime, the cutoff is about 2 p.m.; people used to afternoon coffee need some resolve to skip it.", "plain": "A large coffee six hours before bed cuts that night's sleep by over an hour on the monitor, yet the sleeper's own diary shows no difference. A regular cup needs to be finished almost nine hours before bed to leave sleep alone.", "benefit_text": "400 mg of caffeine 6 hours before bed cut total sleep time measured by instruments by 1.1 to 1.2 hours versus placebo, yet the subjects' own sleep diaries showed no difference — they could not tell. Pooling many studies: caffeine cut total sleep by 45 minutes on average and sleep efficiency by 7%. One cup of coffee (107 mg) had to be finished at least 8.8 hours before bed to leave sleep unaffected.", "sources": "Drake, Roehrs, Shambroom & Roth (2013). Caffeine effects on sleep taken 0, 3, or 6 hours before going to bed. Journal of Clinical Sleep Medicine. <https://doi.org/10.5664/jcsm.3170>; Gardiner et al. (2023). The effect of caffeine on subsequent sleep: A systematic review and meta-analysis. Sleep Medicine Reviews. <https://doi.org/10.1016/j.smrv.2023.101764>", "notes": "The Drake trial is a single lab study using 400 mg — a large dose, about two or three coffees. Slow caffeine metabolizers need an even earlier cutoff. What less caffeine buys back is sleep duration, stacking with item 1 (sleep 7 to 8 hours a night).", "urls": ["https://doi.org/10.1016/j.smrv.2023.101764", "https://doi.org/10.5664/jcsm.3170"]}, {"id": "03-003", "chapter_num": 3, "chapter_title": "Don't waste your energy", "chapter_file": "03-dont-waste-energy.md", "number": 3, "title": "Keep the phone out of the bedroom at night", "money": "0", "time": "low", "willpower": "yes", "benefit": "high", "dimension": "time", "evidence": "B", "grade": "B", "disputed": false, "cost": "Free. The hard part is resisting \"one more episode\" night after night. Keeping the phone out of the bedroom takes the least effort.", "plain": "People who scroll on a phone in bed are more than twice as likely to sleep too little and nearly three times as likely to feel sleepy in the day. A device just sitting in the bedroom, unused, shows nearly the same numbers. The laziest fix is keeping the phone out of the bedroom.", "benefit_text": "Pooling 20 cross-sectional surveys of 125,198 children and adolescents: bedtime users of portable devices were about 2.17 times as likely to sleep too little (odds ratio 2.17), 1.46 times as likely to report poor sleep quality (odds ratio 1.46), and 2.72 times as likely to report daytime sleepiness (odds ratio 2.72). Merely having a device in the bedroom, unused, gave similar figures: about 1.79, 1.53 and 2.27. A separate survey of 844 adults aged 18 to 94: those who still used a phone after lights-out fell asleep more slowly, spent less of their time in bed actually asleep, and felt more tired in the day.", "sources": "Carter et al. (2016). Association Between Portable Screen-Based Media Device Access or Use and Sleep Outcomes. JAMA Pediatrics. <https://doi.org/10.1001/jamapediatrics.2016.2341>; Exelmans & Van den Bulck (2016). Bedtime mobile phone use and sleep in adults. Social Science & Medicine. <https://doi.org/10.1016/j.socscimed.2015.11.037>; Grubbs JB et al. (2018). Pornography Problems Due to Moral Incongruence: An Integrative Model with a Systematic Review and Meta-Analysis. Archives of Sexual Behavior. <https://doi.org/10.1007/s10508-018-1248-x>; Prause N, Pfaus J (2015). Viewing Sexual Stimuli Associated with Greater Sexual Responsiveness, Not Erectile Dysfunction. Sexual Medicine. <https://doi.org/10.1002/sm2.58>", "notes": "Disputed. The cost of staying up late itself is item 1 (sleep 7 to 8 hours a night). Whether porn content itself harms ordinary people's energy or cognition has no reliable causal evidence. One pooled review argues \"porn problems\" come mostly from a mismatch between a person's values and their own behaviour, not from the content itself. Another study pooling 280 men found no link between viewing time and erectile function with a partner. This item targets only the sleep time it displaces.", "urls": ["https://doi.org/10.1001/jamapediatrics.2016.2341", "https://doi.org/10.1002/sm2.58", "https://doi.org/10.1007/s10508-018-1248-x", "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.socscimed.2015.11.037"]}, {"id": "03-004", "chapter_num": 3, "chapter_title": "Don't waste your energy", "chapter_file": "03-dont-waste-energy.md", "number": 4, "title": "Close the door when you do real work; cut interruptions", "money": "0", "time": "low", "willpower": "no", "benefit": "high", "dimension": "time", "evidence": "B", "grade": "B", "disputed": false, "cost": "Free. Close the door, put on headphones, or hang a do-not-disturb sign.", "plain": "An interruption averaging under three seconds doubles the error rate on what comes next; four and a half seconds triples it. Field tracking found it takes about twenty-five minutes to return to the interrupted task, with two other tasks landing in between.", "benefit_text": "An average 2.8-second interruption doubled the step-error rate on sequential tasks; a 4.4-second one tripled it. This was in the lab. A field study of 24 office workers: they switched topics every 11 minutes on average, 57% of topics were interrupted, and returning to the interrupted topic took 25 minutes 26 seconds on average, with 2.26 other tasks in between. A 48-person experiment: interrupted people worked faster, but reported more stress, frustration, time pressure and effort.", "sources": "Altmann, Trafton & Hambrick (2014). Momentary interruptions can derail the train of thought. Journal of Experimental Psychology: General. <https://doi.org/10.1037/a0030986>; Mark, Gonzalez & Harris (2005). No task left behind? Examining the nature of fragmented work. CHI 2005. <https://doi.org/10.1145/1054972.1055017>; Mark, Gudith & Klocke (2008). The cost of interrupted work: More speed and stress. CHI 2008. <https://doi.org/10.1145/1357054.1357072>", "notes": "The field study split interruptions into two kinds: other people coming to you, and yourself opening things. A closed door only blocks the first kind. The second kind needs item 8 (notifications off) and item 10 (batch messages) alongside.", "urls": ["https://doi.org/10.1037/a0030986", "https://doi.org/10.1145/1054972.1055017", "https://doi.org/10.1145/1357054.1357072"]}, {"id": "03-005", "chapter_num": 3, "chapter_title": "Don't waste your energy", "chapter_file": "03-dont-waste-energy.md", "number": 5, "title": "Move your body, get daylight, keep a schedule, call 988 when it gets dark", "money": "0", "time": "mid", "willpower": "some", "benefit": "high", "dimension": "time", "evidence": "A", "grade": "A", "disputed": false, "cost": "Free, or nearly free. A few brisk walks, jogs or strength sessions a week. Daylight during the day. A fixed sleep schedule. One phone call when thoughts turn dark: 988.", "plain": "For low mood, brisk walking and jogging help most, yoga and strength training a little less, and harder beats easier. Light therapy works strongly for seasonal low mood and also for the non-seasonal kind. Guided online self-help courses for low mood work, with a human guide helping more when symptoms are heavier. When thoughts turn to suicide, call 988 first.", "benefit_text": "Pooling 218 randomized trials with 14,170 people (controls doing other activities): walking or jogging against depression g = -0.62 (95% CI -0.80 to -0.45); yoga -0.55; strength training -0.49; harder worked better. Pooling light-therapy studies: seasonal depression 0.84 (0.60 to 1.08), non-seasonal 0.53 (0.18 to 0.89). A patient-level pooled analysis of internet-based cognitive behavioural therapy: guided and unguided both beat controls; with moderate-to-severe symptoms (PHQ-9 above 9), guided worked better. 988 is Canada's national suicide crisis helpline: call or text, English and French, 24 hours a day, 7 days a week, launched November 30, 2023.", "sources": "Noetel M et al. (2024). Effect of exercise for depression: systematic review and network meta-analysis of randomised controlled trials. BMJ. <https://doi.org/10.1136/bmj-2023-075847>; Golden RN et al. (2005). The efficacy of light therapy in the treatment of mood disorders: a review and meta-analysis of the evidence. American Journal of Psychiatry. <https://doi.org/10.1176/appi.ajp.162.4.656>; Karyotaki E et al. (2021). Internet-Based Cognitive Behavioral Therapy for Depression: A Systematic Review and Individual Patient Data Network Meta-analysis. JAMA Psychiatry. <https://doi.org/10.1001/jamapsychiatry.2020.4364>; Government of Canada (2023). 9-8-8 Suicide Crisis Helpline launches. <https://www.canada.ca/en/public-health/news/2023/11/9-8-8-suicide-crisis-helpline.html>; 9-8-8 Suicide Crisis Helpline. <https://988.ca>", "notes": "These are aids for mild-to-moderate low mood. Moderate-to-severe depression, or any suicidal thoughts, need a doctor — call 988 first. Exercise trials were mostly small and cannot blind who exercises. Light-therapy research stops at 2003 and is also small. \"Sleep on schedule\" and \"talk to someone\" are the author's advice, without dedicated citations. Do not turn \"go move\" into a demand on a depressed person; a ten-minute walk counts. 988 operates in English and French only; people who need another language should go through a local crisis line or emergency services.", "urls": ["https://988.ca", "https://doi.org/10.1001/jamapsychiatry.2020.4364", "https://doi.org/10.1136/bmj-2023-075847", "https://doi.org/10.1176/appi.ajp.162.4.656", "https://www.canada.ca/en/public-health/news/2023/11/9-8-8-suicide-crisis-helpline.html"]}, {"id": "03-006", "chapter_num": 3, "chapter_title": "Don't waste your energy", "chapter_file": "03-dont-waste-energy.md", "number": 6, "title": "Quit comparing your life to what people post online", "money": "0", "time": "low", "willpower": "yes", "benefit": "high", "dimension": "time", "evidence": "A", "grade": "A", "disputed": false, "cost": "Free. Setting a limit or deactivating an account takes minutes. The hard part is not opening it again, which takes long-term resolve.", "plain": "People who habitually compare themselves upward online tend to feel worse and think less of themselves. People who deactivated Facebook for four weeks felt slightly better but knew less about current events. Students who capped three platforms at ten minutes a day each felt less lonely and less low after three weeks.", "benefit_text": "Pooling 54 independent samples, 94 effect sizes, N = 36,583: online upward social comparison correlated with poorer psychological adjustment at r = 0.330. Breaking it down: negative evaluative emotions 0.438, anxiety 0.382, depression 0.306, lower well-being 0.268, lower self-esteem 0.263. Age, culture and year did not change the link; long-term studies were no stronger than snapshots. A randomized trial of 2,743 US Facebook users: four weeks deactivated freed 60 minutes a day; subjective well-being rose 0.09 standard deviations (life satisfaction 0.12, anxiety 0.10, depression 0.09, happiness 0.08), about 25% to 40% of a standard positive-psychology intervention; news knowledge fell 0.19 standard deviations. Another randomized trial of 143 undergraduates: capping Facebook, Instagram and Snapchat at 10 minutes a day each cut loneliness and depression after three weeks; anxiety and fear of missing out fell in both groups.", "sources": "Lei Y, Hu S, Sun Y, Zheng L (2026). \"Looking up\" linked to feeling down: a meta-analysis of online upward social comparison and psychological maladjustment. Frontiers in Psychology. <https://doi.org/10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1825169>; Allcott H, Braghieri L, Eichmeyer S, Gentzkow M (2020). The Welfare Effects of Social Media. American Economic Review, 110(3), 629–676. <https://doi.org/10.1257/aer.20190658>; Allcott H, Braghieri L, Eichmeyer S, Gentzkow M (2019). The Welfare Effects of Social Media. NBER Working Paper No. 25514 <https://www.nber.org/papers/w25514>; Hunt MG, Marx R, Lipson C, Young J (2018). No More FOMO: Limiting Social Media Decreases Loneliness and Depression. Journal of Social and Clinical Psychology, 37(10), 751–768. <https://doi.org/10.1521/jscp.2018.37.10.751>", "notes": "The correlation half only shows two things moving together; it cannot be read as cause, and the authors call for more long-term studies. The randomized half can claim cause, but its subjects were US Facebook users before the 2018 midterms — applying it to other platforms is a stretch. The Hunt trial had only 143 undergraduates, and it is the only one. The effects are small; do not expect quitting apps to fix mood. What gets blocked is the \"watching how others are doing\" kind of use — keeping in touch, looking things up, job hunting are not in scope. If willpower fails, start by turning off push, \"people you may know\", and home-page recommendations. The total screen-time math is items 6 and 7 in the \"Don't waste your time\" chapter. For low mood, see item 5 in this chapter. Shopping to fix mood or status is item 24 in the Blacklist chapter; spending to move one rung up is item 28 there.", "urls": ["https://doi.org/10.1257/aer.20190658", "https://doi.org/10.1521/jscp.2018.37.10.751", "https://doi.org/10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1825169", "https://www.nber.org/papers/w25514"]}, {"id": "03-007", "chapter_num": 3, "chapter_title": "Don't waste your energy", "chapter_file": "03-dont-waste-energy.md", "number": 7, "title": "Stop working past about 49 hours a week", "money": "low", "time": "low", "willpower": "some", "benefit": "mid", "dimension": "time", "evidence": "B", "grade": "B", "disputed": false, "cost": "Possibly some overtime pay forgone. It may also take effort to negotiate with a boss.", "plain": "British munitions-factory records from the First World War show output tracking hours up to about forty-nine a week, then flattening: a seventy-hour week produced barely more than a fifty-six-hour one. The extra hours were worked for nothing.", "benefit_text": "Output records of British munitions workers in WWI: up to 49 hours a week, output rose in proportion to hours. Past 49, output growth slowed; it peaked around 63 hours. A 70-hour week produced almost the same as a 56-hour week — the extra 14 hours were worked for nothing.", "sources": "Pencavel (2015). The Productivity of Working Hours. The Economic Journal. <https://doi.org/10.1111/ecoj.12166>", "notes": "The data come from century-old factories — physical work, mostly women. The threshold may differ for knowledge work; the author says other jobs could be higher or lower. What is reliable is the direction: past some point, more hours barely add output. The exact 49 is not reliable.", "urls": ["https://doi.org/10.1111/ecoj.12166"]}, {"id": "03-008", "chapter_num": 3, "chapter_title": "Don't waste your energy", "chapter_file": "03-dont-waste-energy.md", "number": 8, "title": "Turn off notifications; keep the phone out of sight", "money": "0", "time": "low", "willpower": "no", "benefit": "mid", "dimension": "time", "evidence": "B", "grade": "B", "disputed": false, "cost": "Free. A few minutes changing settings; almost no resolve needed.", "plain": "One notification, unanswered and untouched, still drags down scores on attention tasks — roughly matching the cost of actually taking the call. Texting back costs about the same. And merely having your own phone on the desk, successfully ignored, shrinks the mind's working margin. The more phone-dependent the person, the bigger the drop.", "benefit_text": "Merely receiving a notification — not looked at, not touched — measurably worsened attention-task scores, by about as much as actually answering a call or texting. This was in the lab with university students. Two further experiments: even while successfully resisting the phone, having your own phone beside you reduced available cognitive capacity. The most phone-dependent people dropped the most.", "sources": "Stothart, Mitchum & Yehnert (2015). The attentional cost of receiving a cell phone notification. Journal of Experimental Psychology: Human Perception and Performance. <https://doi.org/10.1037/xhp0000100>; Ward, Duke, Gneezy & Bos (2017). Brain Drain: The Mere Presence of One's Own Smartphone Reduces Available Cognitive Capacity. Journal of the Association for Consumer Research. <https://doi.org/10.1086/691462>", "notes": "These are single lab tasks. Nobody has measured what daily accumulation does. For jobs that truly need you on call, keep notifications for a few contacts only.", "urls": ["https://doi.org/10.1037/xhp0000100", "https://doi.org/10.1086/691462"]}, {"id": "03-009", "chapter_num": 3, "chapter_title": "Don't waste your energy", "chapter_file": "03-dont-waste-energy.md", "number": 9, "title": "Wake up at the same time every day", "money": "0", "time": "low", "willpower": "some", "benefit": "mid", "dimension": "time", "evidence": "B", "grade": "B", "disputed": false, "cost": "Free. It takes some resolve to skip the weekend lie-in.", "plain": "The most irregular fifth of sleepers ran about two and a half hours later than the most regular fifth — a permanent jet lag. Students with steadier schedules also did better academically.", "benefit_text": "61 university students logged 30 days. The most irregular fifth had circadian timing about 2.5 hours later than the most regular fifth: melatonin onset 00:08 versus 21:32. Their sleepiest window also ran about 1.8 hours later. Steadier schedules went with better grades (r = 0.37).", "sources": "Phillips et al. (2017). Irregular sleep/wake patterns are associated with poorer academic performance and delayed circadian and sleep/wake timing. Scientific Reports. <https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-017-03171-4>", "notes": "A single small study, no control group. The researchers infer the circadian difference comes mostly from different daily light exposure — so a fixed wake time needs morning light to work. For catching up after an occasional late night, see the \"Don't die slowly\" chapter: go to bed earlier that night instead of sleeping in the next morning.", "urls": ["https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-017-03171-4"]}, {"id": "03-010", "chapter_num": 3, "chapter_title": "Don't waste your energy", "chapter_file": "03-dont-waste-energy.md", "number": 10, "title": "Check email at fixed times; stop living in the inbox", "money": "0", "time": "low", "willpower": "some", "benefit": "mid", "dimension": "time", "evidence": "B", "grade": "B", "disputed": false, "cost": "Free. It takes some time to tell colleagues how often you reply, and some resolve not to peek at email constantly.", "plain": "The same people compared across two weeks: in the week limited to checking email three times a day, daily stress was clearly lower, while the number of emails sent and received stayed the same. Left unlimited, people checked more than fifteen times a day on average.", "benefit_text": "124 adults, two weeks, same people compared with themselves. In the week capped at 3 email checks a day, daily stress was clearly lower than in the unlimited week (Cohen's d = 0.37). Emails received and sent did not differ between the weeks. Unrestricted at baseline, people checked email 15.5 times a day on average.", "sources": "Kushlev & Dunn (2015). Checking email less frequently reduces stress. Computers in Human Behavior. <https://doi.org/10.1016/j.chb.2014.11.005>", "notes": "A single study, medium-to-small effect, measuring stress rather than output. Instant messaging has no direct study; the extension is the author's inference.", "urls": ["https://doi.org/10.1016/j.chb.2014.11.005"]}, {"id": "03-011", "chapter_num": 3, "chapter_title": "Don't waste your energy", "chapter_file": "03-dont-waste-energy.md", "number": 11, "title": "Do one thing at a time; quit \"training\" yourself to multitask", "money": "0", "time": "low", "willpower": "some", "benefit": "mid", "dimension": "time", "evidence": "B", "grade": "B", "disputed": false, "cost": "Free. It takes some resolve not to answer messages mid-meeting.", "plain": "Every task switch makes reactions slower and errors more likely. People who habitually keep several screens going have not trained themselves to multitask; they actually score worse on task-switching tests.", "benefit_text": "After a task switch, reactions are clearly slower and errors more likely — from a review of many studies. In a lab study of university students, heavy media multitaskers were more easily pulled off course by irrelevant things and irrelevant memories, and scored worse on task-switching tests.", "sources": "Monsell (2003). Task switching. Trends in Cognitive Sciences. <https://doi.org/10.1016/S1364-6613(03)00028-7>; Ophir, Nass & Wagner (2009). Cognitive control in media multitaskers. PNAS. <https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.0903620106>", "notes": "How much extra task-switching costs varies a lot by task; no single number is given here. The Ophir study is a same-time-point comparison — it cannot tell whether multitasking worsens attention or people with worse attention multitask more.", "urls": ["https://doi.org/10.1016/S1364-6613(03", "https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.0903620106"]}, {"id": "03-012", "chapter_num": 3, "chapter_title": "Don't waste your energy", "chapter_file": "03-dont-waste-energy.md", "number": 12, "title": "Read paper before bed; dim the screen an hour early", "money": "0", "time": "low", "willpower": "yes", "benefit": "mid", "dimension": "time", "evidence": "B", "grade": "B", "disputed": false, "cost": "Free. Putting the phone down for the last hour before bed takes long-term resolve against habit.", "plain": "Reading on a glowing screen before bed delays sleep by about ten minutes versus paper and pushes the body clock back by over an hour and a half. Melatonin drops by more than half, mornings feel groggier, and full alertness takes longer.", "benefit_text": "In a crossover lab trial, reading a light-emitting e-reader before bed versus a paper book: 25.7 minutes to fall asleep versus 15.8 (about 10 minutes slower). Evening melatonin suppressed by 55%. Next-day circadian timing delayed by over 1.5 hours (melatonin onset 22:31 versus 21:01). Next morning felt sleepier and took longer to fully wake.", "sources": "Chang, Aeschbach, Duffy & Czeisler (2015). Evening use of light-emitting eReaders negatively affects sleep, circadian timing, and next-morning alertness. PNAS. <https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.1418490112>", "notes": "A small lab study under controlled conditions. With everyday phone brightness and usage, the effect may be smaller. Whether night mode helps was not tested.", "urls": ["https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.1418490112"]}, {"id": "03-013", "chapter_num": 3, "chapter_title": "Don't waste your energy", "chapter_file": "03-dont-waste-energy.md", "number": 13, "title": "Keep naps to about 10 minutes", "money": "0", "time": "low", "willpower": "no", "benefit": "mid", "dimension": "time", "evidence": "B", "grade": "B", "disputed": false, "cost": "Free. Ten minutes, plus somewhere to lie down or lean.", "plain": "A ten-minute nap perks people up on the spot, with the lift lasting a couple of hours. Five minutes does almost nothing. Twenty minutes needs half an hour before it pays off. Thirty minutes starts with grogginess, during which performance is worse than no nap at all.", "benefit_text": "After a short night, a 10-minute nap immediately improved sleepiness, fatigue, energy and mental performance, with some benefits lasting past 155 minutes. A 5-minute nap gave almost nothing. A 20-minute nap's benefits appeared only after 35 minutes. A 30-minute nap began with sleep inertia: performance right after waking was worse than no nap, then improved.", "sources": "Brooks & Lack (2006). A brief afternoon nap following nocturnal sleep restriction: Which nap duration is most recuperative? Sleep. <https://doi.org/10.1093/sleep/29.6.831>", "notes": "A single lab study, all healthy young subjects. Do not force it if you cannot sleep. Whether lying with eyes closed for 10 minutes helps has no support. Naps over an hour are linked with higher mortality and heart risk instead — see the \"Don't die slowly\" chapter.", "urls": ["https://doi.org/10.1093/sleep/29.6.831"]}, {"id": "03-014", "chapter_num": 3, "chapter_title": "Don't waste your energy", "chapter_file": "03-dont-waste-energy.md", "number": 14, "title": "Catch yourself replaying the incident; switch to something hands-on", "money": "0", "time": "low", "willpower": "some", "benefit": "mid", "dimension": "time", "evidence": "B", "grade": "B", "disputed": false, "cost": "Free. The hard part is noticing the replay loop in the first place — that noticing takes practice.", "plain": "Replaying the same bad moment never produces an answer. It deepens low mood, cements negative thoughts, weakens problem-solving, blocks real action, and wears out the people willing to listen. Once you catch yourself going in circles, switch to something hands-on.", "benefit_text": "A review of many studies: replaying the same bad moment (rumination) deepens depression. It also entrenches negative thinking, weakens problem-solving, blocks real action, and wears out willing listeners. It is linked with anxiety, binge eating, heavy drinking and self-harm.", "sources": "Nolen-Hoeksema, Wisco & Lyubomirsky (2008). Rethinking Rumination. Perspectives on Psychological Science. <https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1745-6924.2008.00088.x>", "notes": "The review gives directions, not numbers. \"Switch to something hands-on\" is the author's own practice. What the literature supports is one line: diverting attention beats continuing to circle. Nobody has compared what to switch to.", "urls": ["https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1745-6924.2008.00088.x"]}, {"id": "03-015", "chapter_num": 3, "chapter_title": "Don't waste your energy", "chapter_file": "03-dont-waste-energy.md", "number": 15, "title": "Subtract the draining people first, then add the supportive ones", "money": "0", "time": "low", "willpower": "yes", "benefit": "mid", "dimension": "time", "evidence": "C", "grade": "C", "disputed": false, "cost": "Free. There will be short-term social friction and possibly lost relationships in the near term. Refusing takes real resolve.", "plain": "The damage from one draining person outweighs, and is more consistent than, the benefit from one supportive person. So subtract first, then add.", "benefit_text": "A survey of 120 widowed women aged 60 to 89: unpleasant dealings tracked psychological well-being more consistently and more strongly than pleasant ones. By that result, one draining person costs more than one supportive person gives.", "sources": "Rook (1984). The negative side of social interaction: Impact on psychological well-being. Journal of Personality and Social Psychology. <https://doi.org/10.1037/0022-3514.46.5.1097>", "notes": "The respondents are a special group, and it is a one-time survey — no before/after. It measured well-being, not energy. Placing it here is the author's inference. There is no objective standard for which relationships count as \"draining\"; that call is yours.", "urls": ["https://doi.org/10.1037/0022-3514.46.5.1097"]}, {"id": "03-016", "chapter_num": 3, "chapter_title": "Don't waste your energy", "chapter_file": "03-dont-waste-energy.md", "number": 16, "title": "When angry, walk away; do not try to win the argument on the spot", "money": "0", "time": "low", "willpower": "some", "benefit": "mid", "dimension": "time", "evidence": "B", "grade": "B", "disputed": false, "cost": "Free. It takes practice to hold back the retort on the spot, walk away, and decide later whether to act at all.", "plain": "Reframing what just happened — seeing it from the other side — measurably brings feelings down; the second works better. Clamping down on expression does nothing. And people who punished an offender kept replaying the incident longer afterward; those who let it go moved on faster. The satisfaction of revenge is overestimated in advance.", "benefit_text": "Pooling 306 experiments: reframing what happened (cognitive reappraisal) moved emotions at d = 0.36; taking the other side's perspective at d = 0.45; distraction at d = 0.27; suppressing expression did nothing (d = -0.04). Three experiments: people who punished an offender kept replaying it longer; non-punishers turned the page faster. People overestimate revenge's payoff in advance.", "sources": "Webb TL, Miles E, Sheeran P (2012). Dealing with feeling: A meta-analysis of the effectiveness of strategies derived from the process model of emotion regulation. Psychological Bulletin. <https://doi.org/10.1037/a0027600>; Carlsmith KM, Wilson TD, Gilbert DT (2008). The paradoxical consequences of revenge. Journal of Personality and Social Psychology. <https://doi.org/10.1037/a0012165>", "notes": "The pooled part measured emotion intensity, not energy; effects small to medium. The revenge part is a single set of experiments, hence the B. Where someone caused you a real loss, the formal complaint route costs less than exploding on the spot.", "urls": ["https://doi.org/10.1037/a0012165", "https://doi.org/10.1037/a0027600"]}, {"id": "03-017", "chapter_num": 3, "chapter_title": "Don't waste your energy", "chapter_file": "03-dont-waste-energy.md", "number": 17, "title": "Cool anger by calming down, not by venting it", "money": "0", "time": "low", "willpower": "some", "benefit": "mid", "dimension": "time", "evidence": "A", "grade": "A", "disputed": false, "cost": "Free. In the heat of the moment, switch to a few minutes of whatever slows the heartbeat: slow breathing, sitting down, stillness. The hard part is that venting is exactly what you want to do.", "plain": "Punching a bag, smashing things, going for a run — arousing \"let it out\" methods do not reduce anger overall. Calming methods like slow breathing and meditation clearly press down anger and the urge to act on it.", "benefit_text": "Pooling 154 studies, 184 samples, 10,189 people: calming methods (deep breathing, mindfulness, meditation) lowered anger and aggression at g = -0.63 (95% CI -0.82 to -0.43), stable across sex, race, age and culture. Arousing methods (punching bags, jogging, cycling) did nothing overall, g = -0.02 (-0.13 to 0.09). The authors' conclusion: venting anger or \"running it off\" are not working ways to cool down.", "sources": "Kjærvik SL, Bushman BJ (2024). A meta-analytic review of anger management activities that increase or decrease arousal: What fuels or douses rage? Clinical Psychology Review. <https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cpr.2024.102414>", "notes": "Anger and aggression were measured, not energy; the time yardstick is the author's classification — one fewer fight means one fewer mess to clean up. For in-the-moment calming techniques see the chapter on how to rest, and item 16 in this chapter (walk away when angry). Running is not useless: long-term it helps low mood (see the chapter on recovering from a major blow). It is just not an in-the-moment anger tool. Angry: do not drive, do not send messages, do not decide. If someone is hitting you, leave and call the police — that is not an emotion-management problem.", "urls": ["https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cpr.2024.102414"]}, {"id": "03-018", "chapter_num": 3, "chapter_title": "Don't waste your energy", "chapter_file": "03-dont-waste-energy.md", "number": 18, "title": "Make important calls after sleep, not while sleep-deprived", "money": "0", "time": "low", "willpower": "some", "benefit": "low", "dimension": "time", "evidence": "B", "grade": "B", "disputed": false, "cost": "Free. It only pushes things to after sleep.", "plain": "Well-rested people quickly learn to dodge high-risk options. After two straight nights without sleep, people kept picking the risky ones — and the older the person, the stronger the effect. Nobody has measured what a few short hours do, but important calls still belong after sleep.", "benefit_text": "34 healthy people went 49.5 hours without sleep, then did the Iowa Gambling Task, a lab measure of risky choice. Rested, they quickly learned to avoid high-risk options. Sleep-deprived, they chose high-risk options more, and older people were hit harder. One experiment, only one study.", "sources": "Killgore, Balkin & Wesensten (2006). Impaired decision making following 49 h of sleep deprivation. Journal of Sleep Research. <https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1365-2869.2006.00487.x>", "notes": "An extreme case: two straight nights without sleep. What 2 a.m. does to ordinary people was never directly measured. The \"don't send messages\" half is the author's advice (grade C); sending it the next day costs nothing.", "urls": ["https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1365-2869.2006.00487.x"]}, {"id": "03-019", "chapter_num": 3, "chapter_title": "Don't waste your energy", "chapter_file": "03-dont-waste-energy.md", "number": 19, "title": "Wear earplugs or grab a quiet room for focused work", "money": "low", "time": "low", "willpower": "no", "benefit": "low", "dimension": "time", "evidence": "B", "grade": "B", "disputed": false, "cost": "Foam earplugs are cheap. Booking a quiet meeting room takes a little time.", "plain": "Raising background noise from the high thirties to the low fifties of decibels shrinks the words people remember, tires them out, and makes them want to quit. Resting to a nature film with sound restores energy better than resting to office noise.", "benefit_text": "Students worked in a simulated open office. When background noise rose from 39 dB to 51 dB (LAeq), they remembered fewer words, rated themselves more tired, and wanted to work less. Resting to a nature film with sound restored their energy ratings better than river sounds alone or office noise.", "sources": "Jahncke, Hygge, Halin, Green & Dimberg (2011). Open-plan office noise: Cognitive performance and restoration. Journal of Environmental Psychology. <https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jenvp.2011.07.002>", "notes": "A single lab study. The most disruptive noise is intelligible speech. White-noise earphones help with that, but this study did not test earphones.", "urls": ["https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jenvp.2011.07.002"]}, {"id": "03-020", "chapter_num": 3, "chapter_title": "Don't waste your energy", "chapter_file": "03-dont-waste-energy.md", "number": 20, "title": "Stop treating a pessimistic forecast as an accurate prediction", "money": "0", "time": "low", "willpower": "some", "benefit": "low", "dimension": "death", "evidence": "B (disputed)", "grade": "B", "disputed": true, "cost": "Free. The hard part is treating this kind of thought as a symptom rather than as fact.", "plain": "In a twenty-year twin study, each step up in pessimism came with about a tenth more deaths in the same period and about a fifth more cardiovascular deaths. Higher optimism scores brought no matching benefit. So the thing to handle is pessimism, not a lack of positivity.", "benefit_text": "2,978 Australian twins aged 50 and up, followed 20 years on average; 1,068 died. Each standard deviation higher on the pessimism scale came with about 13% higher mortality in the same period (hazard ratio 1.134, 95% CI 1.065-1.207) and about 20% higher cardiovascular death (1.196, 1.045-1.368). No link with cancer death. Optimism scores showed no clear link with mortality either.", "sources": "Whitfield JB, Zhu G, Landers JG, Martin NG (2020). Pessimism is associated with greater all-cause and cardiovascular mortality, but optimism is not protective. Scientific Reports, 10, 12609. <https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-020-69388-y>", "notes": "Disputed. This is observation only, no grouping. One could argue sickness came first and caused the pessimism; the authors counter that cancer deaths should then also correlate. Unmeasured factors may still remain. Optimism showed no protective effect, so do not force \"positive thinking\". The work is to stop treating pessimistic forecasts as accurate predictions of the future.", "urls": ["https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-020-69388-y"]}, {"id": "03-021", "chapter_num": 3, "chapter_title": "Don't waste your energy", "chapter_file": "03-dont-waste-energy.md", "number": 21, "title": "Fix the trivial defaults: what to wear, what to eat", "money": "0", "time": "low", "willpower": "no", "benefit": "low", "dimension": "time", "evidence": "C (disputed)", "grade": "C", "disputed": true, "cost": "Free. It takes a little time up front to fix the defaults.", "plain": "There is no hard evidence for this one. The original \"choices drain willpower\" experiment failed to replicate in two large pre-registered redos — one with about two thousand people, one with about three thousand — across many labs, with effects near zero. Fixing what you wear and eat is still worth doing, for the minutes and the dithering it saves, not for \"saving willpower.\"", "benefit_text": "Cannot be quantified. The original experiment claimed choosing and self-control draw on one shared limited resource (ego depletion). Two later large pre-registered replications, run as originally designed across many labs, did not reproduce it: 23 labs and 2,141 people, d = 0.04 (95% CI -0.07 to 0.15); 36 labs and 3,531 people, d = 0.06 — too small to count.", "sources": "Baumeister, Bratslavsky, Muraven & Tice (1998). Ego depletion: Is the active self a limited resource? Journal of Personality and Social Psychology. <https://doi.org/10.1037/0022-3514.74.5.1252>; Hagger et al. (2016). A Multilab Preregistered Replication of the Ego-Depletion Effect. Perspectives on Psychological Science. <https://doi.org/10.1177/1745691616652873>; Vohs et al. (2021). A Multisite Preregistered Paradigmatic Test of the Ego-Depletion Effect. Psychological Science. <https://doi.org/10.1177/0956797621989733>", "notes": "Disputed. \"Decision fatigue\" is treated as settled in popular books, but later direct replications mostly failed. It is still worth doing: near-zero cost, and the minutes saved are real. Do not expect it to improve your afternoon judgment.", "urls": ["https://doi.org/10.1037/0022-3514.74.5.1252", "https://doi.org/10.1177/0956797621989733", "https://doi.org/10.1177/1745691616652873"]}, {"id": "03-022", "chapter_num": 3, "chapter_title": "Don't waste your energy", "chapter_file": "03-dont-waste-energy.md", "number": 22, "title": "Treat police officers, doctors and counter staff as people working by the rules, not as roles", "money": "0", "time": "low", "willpower": "some", "benefit": "low", "dimension": "time", "evidence": "C", "grade": "C", "disputed": false, "cost": "Free. What changes is your expectation and the order of your actions. The first time, you have to resist the urge to argue until the other side agrees.", "plain": "Police officers, doctors and counter staff are people working a post by the rules, managed by paperwork, deadlines and oversight. Whether they are nice to you has little to do with whether your matter moves and how many days it takes. So get receipts, note deadlines, and use the proper channel — instead of trying to argue someone into agreement.", "benefit_text": "Swap the moves: from pleading, anger and re-telling the grievance, to getting documents, noting deadlines, and appealing through the proper channel. What is saved is repeated back-and-forth and replaying it afterward. This is a one-time adjustment of expectations, not daily time saved.", "sources": "Ontario Ombudsman. What to gather before making a complaint. <https://www.ombudsman.on.ca/en/make-complaint/what-you-can-expect/how-make-complaint>", "notes": "Rated C because the claim that people treat counter staff as roles rather than workers has no citable literature. What can be cited is the procedural half: the Ontario Ombudsman's guide lists what to gather before complaining — names and titles, dates, and written communication. A paper trail does not fix a rude attitude and does not guarantee the outcome; it changes your expectation and your first move.", "urls": ["https://www.ombudsman.on.ca/en/make-complaint/what-you-can-expect/how-make-complaint"]}, {"id": "03-023", "chapter_num": 3, "chapter_title": "Don't waste your energy", "chapter_file": "03-dont-waste-energy.md", "number": 23, "title": "Halve your estimate when it feels like everyone saw you embarrass yourself", "money": "0", "time": "low", "willpower": "some", "benefit": "low", "dimension": "time", "evidence": "B", "grade": "B", "disputed": false, "cost": "Free. Just one extra question in the tense moment: did they really notice.", "plain": "Wearing an embarrassing T-shirt into a room, the wearer guessed nearly half the onlookers could name the face on it; under a quarter actually could. Wearing a T-shirt they felt good in, people guessed six times the true number. After conversations, people also systematically underestimate how much the other person liked them. So when it feels like everyone remembers your embarrassment, even half your estimate is still too high.", "benefit_text": "Experiment one: participants wore a T-shirt with an embarrassing face into a room of onlookers. Wearers estimated 46% of onlookers could name the face; the actual rate was 23% — the estimate was exactly double. Experiment two: wearing a T-shirt they chose and felt good in, wearers estimated 48% while the actual rate was 8% — six times. A separate line of research across three settings — strangers paired for conversation, freshman roommates, adult workshops — found people systematically underestimate how much their conversation partner liked them and enjoyed the conversation: the \"liking gap\". It appeared at every conversation length and lasted months between roommates.", "sources": "Gilovich T, Medvec VH, Savitsky K (2000). The spotlight effect in social judgment: An egocentric bias in estimates of the salience of one's own actions and appearance. Journal of Personality and Social Psychology, 78(2), 211–222. <https://doi.org/10.1037/0022-3514.78.2.211>; Boothby EJ, Cooney G, Sandstrom GM, Clark MS (2018). The Liking Gap in Conversations: Do People Like Us More Than We Think? Psychological Science, 29(11), 1742–1756. <https://doi.org/10.1177/0956797618783714>", "notes": "Rated B because these are small lab and campus studies — T-shirts and chats with strangers. There is no direct evidence for \"colleagues gossiping about me\" or \"misspeaking in a meeting\". The numbers show the direction and the rough multiple, not a coefficient to apply. Other people's views are not all unimportant: in real evaluations — interviews, exams, performance reviews — the judgment is real, so prepare for those. What gets discounted is the everyday feeling that everyone is watching; that part is your own estimation bias. Where someone caused you a real loss, the formal complaint route costs less than exploding on the spot.", "urls": ["https://doi.org/10.1037/0022-3514.78.2.211", "https://doi.org/10.1177/0956797618783714"]}]);
